<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<!-- The languages Isleta speaks, and the switch that turns the packages on.

	     This list is load-bearing in a way that is invisible when it is wrong. Measured on macOS
	     27.0 with a probe .app carrying a package-style resource bundle: CFBundle negotiates a
	     *nested* bundle's language against the **main** bundle's list, so with `de` absent here,
	     `Bundle.module.preferredLocalizations` inside IslandUI, IslandSettings, IslandActivities
	     and IslandSources all collapse to ["en"] and every one of them silently draws English —
	     no warning, no exception, nothing in any log. Adding `de` here (with no de.lproj in the
	     app at all) was enough on its own to make all four resolve.

	     So: a language ships when it is in this array. Removing one here switches it off across
	     the whole app, including in packages whose .lproj folders are still present and correct.

	     `en` is listed although there is no en.lproj anywhere — English is the `defaultValue`
	     argument at each call site, which is where the copy is argued and where it stays. -->
	<key>CFBundleLocalizations</key>
	<array>
		<string>en</string>
		<string>de</string>
		<string>fr</string>
		<string>es</string>
	</array>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>2.1.0</string>
	<!-- Must equal CFBundleShortVersionString above. Sparkle compares CFBundleVersion, not the
	     marketing string, so if this does not increase every existing install silently concludes
	     it is up to date — a failure that logs nothing anywhere. Tools/release.sh checks the two
	     rather than syncing them, because PlistBuddy would rewrite this file and strip these
	     comments. -->
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>2.1.0</string>
	<key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
	<string>$(MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET)</string>
	<key>NSHumanReadableCopyright</key>
	<string>Copyright © 2026 Isleta. All rights reserved.</string>
	<!-- Required, not optional: the Now Playing scripting route asks Music and Spotify what is
	     playing through /usr/bin/osascript, and TCC attributes that Apple event to Isleta as the
	     responsible process. Without this key macOS refuses the event outright (errAEEventNotPermitted)
	     and there is no dialog and no log line saying why — the route simply never returns a track.
	     There is deliberately no Accessibility key here: macOS has none. Accessibility is granted
	     through AXIsProcessTrustedWithOptions and the System Settings list, and no Info.plist string
	     is consulted for it. -->
	<key>NSAppleEventsUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Isleta asks your music app what’s playing so it can show the current track in the island when Isleta starts. Live updates need no permission.</string>
	<!-- Required, and 1.3.0 was cut without it. The Bluetooth device source registers for
	     IOBluetooth's connect notification, which spins up CoreBluetooth's coordinator underneath,
	     and that files a TCC access request. TCC kills any process that makes one with no usage
	     string: SIGABRT inside __TCC_CRASHING_DUE_TO_PRIVACY_VIOLATION__, about 270 ms after
	     launch, with the island never drawn and nothing in our own log to say why — the last line
	     written is the timer source starting.

	     The trap is that it cannot be reproduced the way this project verifies everything else. TCC
	     attributes a request to the *responsible* process, so a build launched from a shell — which
	     is what --perf-report, --device-demo and every hardware check do — is judged against
	     Terminal's usage strings and Terminal's grant, and runs perfectly for as long as you watch
	     it. Only a launch through LaunchServices (Finder, Dock, login item) makes Isleta its own
	     responsible process. Verify anything permission-shaped with `open -a Isleta`, never from
	     the shell. -->
	<key>NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Isleta shows your AirPods and other Bluetooth audio devices in the island when they connect, with the charge left in them. Nothing about your devices leaves your Mac.</string>
	<!-- Calendar, for the glance. Deliberately the *FullAccess* key and not the legacy
	     NSCalendarsUsageDescription: write-only access authorises saving new items and returns no
	     calendars to read, which is the opposite of what a "what's next" surface needs.

	     Measured on macOS 27 with three bundled probes, each launched with `open` so each was its
	     own responsible process. The legacy key does still work — tccd carries all three names and
	     falls back — but that is a fallback rather than a licence. The case that matters is having
	     *no* calendar key at all, which does not abort the way the Bluetooth one above does: the
	     request answers granted=false with a nil error in 9 ms, raises no prompt, logs nothing, and
	     leaves authorizationStatus at notDetermined rather than denied. A source that retries on
	     notDetermined therefore asks forever and never advances, and the whole signal is nine
	     milliseconds and a false-looking false. -->
	<key>NSCalendarsFullAccessUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Isleta reads your upcoming events so it can show what’s next in the island. Your events are never stored, never sent anywhere, and never written to Isleta’s logs.</string>
	<!-- Location, for the weather. Optional by design — WeatherKit takes a CLLocation and has no
	     opinion about where it came from, so a city typed in Settings gives a fully working weather
	     island with this permission refused or never asked for.

	     Unlike every other permission in this file, location is **not a TCC service** —
	     kTCCServiceLocation is absent from tccd's table and authorization lives in
	     /var/db/locationd/clients.plist. So a missing usage string here does not abort the process:
	     measured, a probe with no location key still raised a prompt and was granted. That makes
	     the string invisible in testing and load-bearing in production, because without it the user
	     is shown a prompt with no reason on it. It is the WhenInUse key and not the Always one:
	     Always asks for background wake-ups an LSUIElement agent has no use for. -->
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Isleta uses your location to show the weather where you are. You can type a city instead — and either way, your location is only ever sent to Apple’s weather service.</string>
	<!-- Focus, for the gate rather than for a feature. Isleta reads whether a Focus is on so it can
	     decline to put an island on screen while the user has asked for quiet — it is consulted once
	     when an activity is about to be published, never on a timer, and it drives no activity of
	     its own.

	     Two measurements decided the shape. There is **no change notification anywhere**:
	     INFocusStatusCenter.h declares two properties and one method and no constant, and neither
	     Intents nor DoNotDisturb nor Focus.framework carries a Darwin name for state — only private
	     XPC. So a live Focus indicator would need a poll, and is not shipped. And `isFocused`
	     answers a definite Optional(false) — not nil — whether or not authorization was ever
	     granted, so an unauthorised read is indistinguishable from "no Focus is on". Every read is
	     gated on authorizationStatus == .authorized, or the island would tell a user sitting in Do
	     Not Disturb, with total confidence, that they are not. -->
	<key>NSFocusStatusUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Isleta checks whether a Focus is on so it can stay quiet when you have asked your Mac for quiet. It never changes your Focus, and it never records it.</string>
	<key>LSUIElement</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Sparkle (§8.1.10). The appcast is committed to the repo and served raw from GitHub, which
	     gives it a URL that never changes between releases. https only: Sparkle will refuse a
	     plain-http feed without an App Transport Security exception, and adding one would be
	     weakening update security to save a redirect. -->
	<key>SUFeedURL</key>
	<string>https://raw.githubusercontent.com/idevtim/isleta/main/appcast.xml</string>
	<!-- The public half of Isleta's EdDSA (ed25519) key pair, generated 2026-08-19. The private
	     half lives in the release machine's login keychain and a password manager, and never in
	     this repo — `Tools/release.sh` is the only thing that touches it, through
	     `generate_appcast`, which signs each archive without printing it.

	     Do not edit this string. Every copy of Isleta already installed verifies updates against
	     the key that shipped inside it, so changing this key strands them: a new pair does not
	     rescue an install that trusts the old one, and losing the private half strands them just
	     the same. Never paste another app's key here either — one leaked private key must not sign
	     updates for two applications.

	     Blanking it is a safe failure rather than a broken build: `-[SPUUpdater startUpdater:]`
	     validates this value before it will start, so a build without a valid key cannot check for
	     updates at all, and cannot be talked into installing anything by a spoofed feed.
	     `SparkleUpdater` turns that refusal into `canCheckForUpdates == false`, which the settings
	     window states out loud. `Tools/release.sh` refuses to build in that state. -->
	<key>SUPublicEDKey</key>
	<string>TDnC5yMZcwQ8x4jjkJFyxK7yJF+z34NhMk4BanAiij0=</string>
	<!-- The shipped default, matching `IsletaConfiguration.automaticUpdateChecks`. Whatever the user
	     has actually chosen is pushed into the updater before it starts, so this value is only ever
	     read on a machine that has never opened the settings window. Its other job is to tell
	     Sparkle the question has been answered, which suppresses Sparkle's own "check for updates
	     automatically?" permission prompt — Isleta asks that in its own settings window, and two
	     dialogs asking the same question can disagree. -->
	<key>SUEnableAutomaticChecks</key>
	<true/>
	<key>NSSupportsAutomaticTermination</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSSupportsSuddenTermination</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
